From 728fc9f85343919565950ff84ee7fa9b99bec913 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: J P <jpwatters@mac.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 08:21:10 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 42/42] nas-relay: answer Find NAS from the interface the query
 arrived on

The relay answered "QMXR?" with, and from, whatever interface the routing
table picks for the asker. With DSM's VPN client connected (tun0,
192.168.113.5) and pushing 10.0.0.0/24 - inside the LAN's own 10.0.0.0/22 -
that was the tunnel: a Tab5 at 10.0.0.28 was answered "QMXR! 192.168.113.5
7355" into the VPN and Find NAS found nothing, while TCP to 10.0.0.137 kept
working (DSM's rule "from 10.0.0.137 lookup eth0-table" sends replies from
the LAN address out of eth0).

The responder now turns on IP_PKTINFO and answers with the address of the
interface the query came in on (the destination address itself for a
unicast query), sent from that address and interface, so DSM's source rule
applies. in_pktinfo.ipi_spec_dst is not used for broadcasts: Linux fills it
from a route lookup back to the sender and it said 192.168.113.5 too
(measured on the DS416j, 2026-10-31). Without IP_PKTINFO it falls back to
the routing table as before. The log line says which was used and notes
when the routing table would have chosen another address.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W7MAsxUnuCnfwkHxdqp61S
